---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="How to choose an AI coding plan | Awesome Coding Plans" description="A decision guide for picking an AI coding subscription by budget, usage style, and tool surface.">
  <section class="guide reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">guide</p>
    <h1>How to <span>choose.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">Four questions separate a good subscription from shelfware. Work through them in order.</p>

    <section class="step">
      <h2>1. Where do you actually code?</h2>
      <p>If you live in an IDE, a plan whose value is editor-integrated (Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot, Qoder) fits. If you work in the terminal, prefer CLI-first plans (OpenCode Go, GLM, Kimi Code, Codex). If you wire your own harness, you want API-style access or BYOK (OpenRouter, Chutes, Perch, Synthetic).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="step">
      <h2>2. How heavy is your usage?</h2>
      <p>Light (a few completions and chats a day): free tiers cover you. Daily driver: a $10-$20 tier is the sweet spot, and quota mechanics matter more than the headline price. Heavy or bursty agentic use: check the fine print for 5-hour and weekly windows before trusting a "premium requests" number.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="step">
      <h2>3. Which models do you need?</h2>
      <p>Frontier-only plans (Claude, Codex, Gemini) bet on one lab. Multi-model and aggregator plans trade some integration polish for the freedom to route each task to the right model, often with your own API keys at provider prices.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="step">
      <h2>4. Who pays, and for how many seats?</h2>
      <p>Solo tiers are priced per person. Team and enterprise plans add pooled quotas, admin controls, and indemnity, and are usually negotiated rather than listed. Students should check the student page before paying anything.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="step">
      <h2>Quick decision table</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>If you are...</th><th>Start with</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A student</td><td><a href="/student-discounts/">Student discounts</a> first, then free tiers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>On a hard budget</td><td>Budget band under $10: <a href="/by-price/">browse by price</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Locked to one editor</td><td>IDE-tagged plans: <a href="/tools/">browse by tool</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Routing many models</td><td>Aggregators and BYOK: <a href="/by-model-access/">browse by model access</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Buying for a team</td><td>Team/enterprise tags: <a href="/by-tag/">browse by tag</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .guide { max-width: 820px; padding: 4rem 0 2rem; }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--accent); font: 500 .72rem "DM Mono", monospace; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem); line-height: .95; letter-spacing: -.07em; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  h1 span { color: var(--accent); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 620px; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .step { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .step h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
  .step p { margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 650px) { table { display: block; overflow-x: auto; white-space: nowrap; } }
</style>
